  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 228 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nA cold front moved across the plains early this morning, which \nwill bring a marked change to the weather. Cooler temperatures \ncan be expected in all areas east of the Front Range mountains, \nwith highs mostly in the upper 60s to lower 70s. There is still \nsome uncertainty with the amount of cooling, as models vary \nconsiderably on the low level moisture and stratus potential. At \nthis point, we do think we'll see at least areas of stratus this \nmorning, with the highest concentration generally Denver and\nBoulder to points south and southeast. Given the somewhat limited\nextent, we do expect that to break up by late morning although \nmid level clouds could slow that dissipation and warming. As a \nresult, we'll lean toward the cooler side of any short term \nguidance. With regard to shower and storm chances, a few showers \nand even a couple rumbles of thunder continues over the mountains \nearly this morning. Those should gradually dissipate as we lose \nany upper level support (right entrance region of the upper jet). \nHowever, sufficient daytime heating over the mountains will allow \nscattered showers and a few storms to redevelop this afternoon. \nThe plains will be much more stable, making it difficult for any \nshowers drifting off the mountains to survive very far east onto \nthe I-25 Corridor and adjacent plains.\n\nFor Wednesday, we'll see flat upper level ridging which will allow\ntemperatures to warm to above normal readings again (mid 80s for \nmost of the plains). Instability will increase slightly, with a \nfew hundred J/kg of MLCAPE. Most convective initiation will once \nagain be over the mountains, but given improving thermodynamic \nprofiles we should see eventual development and movement of \nscattered showers and storms into the I-25 Corridor and adjacent \nplains through the late afternoon and evening hours. Some of these\nshowers and storms will likely continue overnight given further \nmoisture and mid level theta-e advection. \n\nThursday is still shaping up to be a more active weather day. Two\nreasons for this; Precipitable water values climbing to 200% of\nnormal and upper level support from a disturbance originating from\nthe northeastern tropical Pacific (currently near the southern \ntip of the Baja California peninsula, or vicinity of Cabo San \nLucas). However, timing of this feature may be key in potential \nconvective coverage and intensity, as too early of an arrival \n(like the GFS) could mean we'll just see scattered and lighter \nshowers and a few storms. A later arrival during peak heating \ncould mean more numerous showers and storms with locally heavy \nrainfall. While the EC and GEM are a little slower than the GFS, \nthey could still be a bit on the early side. In any case, \nsomething to monitor over the next couple days as warm cloud \ndepths are forecast to increase to 3,500-6,000 feet, fairly \nimpressive for this time of year. \n\nThe weather still looks fairly active into Friday. While there is\nless moisture, there is still some upper level support due to a \ntrough drifting slowly east across the Northern Rockies and\nnorthern Great Basin. Timing is uncertain here, but appears\nSaturday could still be quite active before drier weather more \nlikely arrives late in the weekend. Temperatures will be holding \nnear or even slightly below normal for this period.",
